---
import Solution from '../../layouts/Solution.astro';
import MinimizationFunnel from '../../components/MinimizationFunnel.astro';
import SolutionFit from '../../components/SolutionFit.astro';
import SolutionFaq from '../../components/SolutionFaq.astro';
import DocsHandoff from '../../components/DocsHandoff.astro';
import { solutionAreas } from '../../data/solutions';
import { products } from '../../data/products';
const area = solutionAreas.find((a) => a.key === 'check-a-fact')!;
const evidence = products.find((p) => p.key === 'evidence-gateway')!;
const relay = products.find((p) => p.key === 'protected-registry-apis')!;
---
<Solution
  area={area}
  heading="Let a service check a fact without receiving the record."
  description="Other services ask your office for copies of records when all they need is one fact: whether a person is alive, whether an applicant is 18 or older, whether a business is registered. Registry Stack lets those services ask your register that one question and receive only the answer, while the record stays with your office."
>
  <section id="example" class="section section-muted" aria-labelledby="check-example-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading center">
        <p class="eyebrow">One example: a civil register</p>
        <h2 id="check-example-title">One register answers several services without sending the record.</h2>
        <p>A pension service asks whether a person is alive. A licensing service asks whether they are 18 or older. Your office decides which questions each service may ask. A question it has not agreed to is refused, and nothing from the record is sent.</p>
      </div>
      <MinimizationFunnel />
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="check-change-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">What changes for your office</p>
        <h2 id="check-change-title">Fewer copies leave your office, and each answer can be checked.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="product-facts">
        <article><h3>A service gets the answer, not the file</h3><p>Your office agrees with each service what a question means and how it is worked out from the record. A service can learn the region a person lives in without the street address, or that they are an adult without their date of birth.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Only the services you agreed with can ask</h3><p>Each service may ask the questions agreed with it, for the reason agreed with it. Anything else is refused. Every question and its outcome are kept, so your office can show who asked what, and when.</p></article>
        <article><h3>The receiving service can trust the answer without calling you</h3><p>Your office sets how long an answer holds. Before relying on it, the receiving service checks that the answer came from your register, was not altered, and is still in date.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section section-muted" aria-labelledby="check-ai-title">
    <div class="section-inner product-fit">
      <div class="section-heading compact">
        <p class="eyebrow">AI-assisted services</p>
        <h2 id="check-ai-title">An AI-assisted service asks under the same rules.</h2>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>Some services use AI to guide an applicant or a caseworker. Such a service asks your register the way any other service does: it may ask only the questions agreed with it, and it gets only the answer.</p>
        <p>The choice is the same as for any other service: send copies of the records, or answer the question. A housing benefit service could receive each applicant’s income records and let its application work them out. Or your office agrees one question, such as whether annual net income is below the benefit threshold, and your register returns that answer. If your register holds gross monthly income and the rule needs net annual income, the agreed question carries that conversion, and the AI-assisted service never holds the record.</p>
        <p>The service that asks stays responsible for what it does with the answer, including decisions about eligibility and approvals, and how it treats the people it asks about.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <SolutionFit
    area={area}
    heading="Give a service a checked answer, or the fields it needs."
    jobs={{
      'evidence-gateway': 'Use it when the other service needs a yes or no, or a status, such as whether a person is an adult or a business is registered: it gets that answer and can check it came from your register and was not altered.',
      'protected-registry-apis': 'Use it when the other service has to work with the values themselves, such as a business’s legal name and legal form, or a farmer’s registered status and farm size: it gets the fields your office agreed, and no others.',
    }}
    extra={[
      { term: 'Your register stays where it is', body: 'Both work from a register your office already runs, and neither requires Base Registry Engine. If you use both, your technical team sets up the connection to your register.' },
    ]}
  >
    <p>Start from what the other service has to decide, and give it the least that decision needs. A good first question is the one your office is asked for most often.</p>
  </SolutionFit>
  <SolutionFaq
    muted
    items={[
      {
        q: 'Do we have to move our register?',
        a: 'No. Both products read from the register your office already runs. Your staff keep recording and correcting records where they do today, and the answers reflect what the register holds.',
        link: { href: 'https://docs.registrystack.org/explanation/integration-patterns/', label: 'How it sits beside existing systems' },
      },
      {
        q: 'How does the receiving service know an answer is genuine?',
        a: 'Each answer carries a digital signature from your register and the dates it is valid for. The receiving service checks both itself, so it does not need to call your office to confirm the answer.',
        link: { href: evidence.href, label: `About ${evidence.name}` },
      },
      {
        q: 'Does this replace our data-sharing agreements?',
        a: 'No. The agreement still says what a service may know and why. The questions and fields your office sets up follow that agreement, and a request outside it is refused and kept on record.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Where does our technical team start?',
        a: `With the ${evidence.name} tutorial, which answers a yes-or-no question from sample data and checks the answer, on a laptop. If a service needs the values themselves, the ${relay.name} tutorial is the next step.`,
        link: { href: evidence.tutorialHref, label: evidence.tutorialLabel },
      },
      {
        q: 'And if a service needs the values themselves?',
        a: `The ${relay.name} tutorial returns two agreed fields from a sample register, refuses a third, and shows the record of each request.`,
        link: { href: relay.tutorialHref, label: relay.tutorialLabel },
      },
    ]}
  />
  <DocsHandoff eyebrow="Work with us" heading="Get help when you need it." body="Get hands-on help through a paid implementation pilot, or ongoing support while your team agrees the questions and connects the register." emphasis="pilot" />
</Solution>
